2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] You're right! On Amazon Linux 2, you can use the instance metadata service directly. Here's the
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] updated `ec2-boot.sh`:
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] #!/bin/bash
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] # AWS Instance Identification (no curl needed)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] INSTANCE_ID=$(TOKEN=`curl -X PUT "http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token" -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600"` && curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] INSTANCE_TYPE=$(TOKEN=`curl -X PUT "http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token" -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600"` && curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-type)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] # System Identification
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] HOSTNAME=$(hostname)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] SWAP_FILE="/swapfile"
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] # Log startup with identifiers
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] echo "Starting EC2 Boot: $INSTANCE_ID ($INSTANCE_TYPE) on $HOSTNAME" >> /var/log/ec2-boot.log
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] # Your existing commands
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] sudo swapon $SWAP_FILE
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] sudo hostname -b sydney
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] sudo systemctl start redis6
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] sudo service nginx start
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] pushd dsptool/native/bin
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] echo "Starting fit3 on $INSTANCE_ID" >> /var/log/ec2-boot.log
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] ./fit3
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] popd
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] • Note*: Amazon Linux 2023 (which you're running) requires the IMDSv2 token, hence the more
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] verbose syntax. If you want simpler syntax, you could use:
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] # Alternative - set IMDSv2 token once
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] TOKEN=$(curl -X PUT "http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token" -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600" 2>/dev/null)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] INSTANCE_ID=$(curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id 2>/dev/null)
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:56:16 [deepseek-chat] This gives you the AWS instance identifiers without external dependencies.
