2025-10-01 07:57:01 [ec2-user@skidnz] is there an aws or ec2 command line app i could install
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] Yes! AWS provides the AWS CLI which you can install:
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] Install AWS CLI on Amazon Linux 2
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # Install AWS CLI v2 (recommended)
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] unzip awscliv2.zip
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo ./aws/install
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # Or using package manager
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo yum install -y aws-cli
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] Then your ec2-boot.sh becomes:
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] #!/bin/bash
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # AWS Instance Identification using AWS CLI
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] INSTANCE_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids $(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id) --query 'Reservations[0].Instances[0].InstanceId' --output text)
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] INSTANCE_TYPE=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids $(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id) --query 'Reservations[0].Instances[0].InstanceType' --output text)
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] REGION=$(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/region)
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # Get instance tags
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] ENVIRONMENT=$(aws ec2 describe-tags --region $REGION --filters "Name=resource-id,Values=$INSTANCE_ID" "Name=key,Values=Environment" --query 'Tags[0].Value' --output text)
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # Your existing commands
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo swapon /swapfile
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo hostname -b sydney
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo systemctl start redis6
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] sudo service nginx start
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] echo "Starting $INSTANCE_ID ($INSTANCE_TYPE) - $ENVIRONMENT" >> /var/log/ec2-boot.log
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] pushd dsptool/native/bin
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] ./fit3
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] popd
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] Configure AWS CLI first:
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] aws configure
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] # Enter your AWS Access Key, Secret Key, Region, and output format
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-10-01 07:57:21 [deepseek-chat] The AWS CLI gives you much richer instance metadata and control than the basic metadata service.
